---
title: Sensors
description: Pluggable input sensors — configure PointerSensor and KeyboardSensor or write your own.
---

# Sensors

Sensors are the layer that translates raw input events (pointer, keyboard, touch) into drag lifecycle calls. The library ships with two built-in sensors and exposes the `SensorDescriptor` interface so you can write your own.

## Built-in sensors

| Sensor | Activates on | Notes |
|--------|-------------|-------|
| `PointerSensor` | `pointerdown` | Handles mouse, touch, and stylus. Respects `dragDelay` and `scrollCancelThreshold` |
| `KeyboardSensor` | `Enter` / `Space` | Navigate zones with arrow keys, confirm with Enter, cancel with Escape |

Both are exported from `@horuse/svelte-dnd`.

## Default behaviour

If you do not configure `sensors` explicitly, the controller uses `[PointerSensor, KeyboardSensor]`.

## Configuring sensors

Pass sensors to `DndController`:

```ts
import {
    DndController,
    PointerSensor,
    KeyboardSensor,
} from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

const controller = new DndController({
    sensors: [new PointerSensor(), new KeyboardSensor(controller)],
});
```

> **Note:** `KeyboardSensor` needs a reference to the controller to read `filteredDropZones` for navigation. Pass it after constructing the controller.

## Pointer-only (disable keyboard)

```ts
const controller = new DndController({
    sensors: [new PointerSensor()],
});
```

## Per-item sensor override

You can override sensors on individual draggable items using the `sensors` prop on `DndDraggable`:

```svelte
<DndDraggable id={item.id} {position} sensors={[new PointerSensor()]}>
    {item.label}
</DndDraggable>
```

The per-item sensors take precedence over controller-level sensors for that element.

## PointerSensor options

`PointerSensor` respects the `dragDelay` and `scrollCancelThreshold` values passed to `DndDraggable` (or the `draggable` action):

| Option | Default | Description |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| `dragDelay` | `300` ms | On touch devices: wait this long before deciding the gesture is a drag rather than a scroll |
| `scrollCancelThreshold` | `8` px | If the finger moves more than this during `dragDelay`, treat it as a scroll and cancel drag activation |

## KeyboardSensor navigation

Once activated (Enter or Space on a focused draggable), keyboard navigation works as follows:

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| `ArrowDown` / `ArrowRight` | Move to next position in current container |
| `ArrowUp` / `ArrowLeft` | Move to previous position in current container |
| `ArrowRight` (at last item) | Jump to first position in next container |
| `ArrowLeft` (at first item) | Jump to last position in previous container |
| `Enter` | Confirm drop at current preview position |
| `Escape` | Cancel drag, ghost returns to origin |

## Custom sensor

Implement `SensorDescriptor` to create your own input sensor:

```ts
import type { SensorDescriptor, SensorActivation, SensorOptions, SensorCallbacks } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

const mySensor: SensorDescriptor = {
    activate(event, element, options, callbacks): SensorActivation | null {
        // Return null if this sensor doesn't handle the event
        if (!(event instanceof CustomEvent)) return null;

        // Start drag immediately
        callbacks.onStart({ x: 0, y: 0 }, element);

        return {
            initialTransform: { x: 0, y: 0 },
            offset: { x: 0, y: 0 },
            destroy() {
                // Clean up any listeners added here
            },
        };
    },
};

const controller = new DndController({ sensors: [mySensor] });
```

## Types

```ts
interface SensorDescriptor {
    activate(
        event: Event,
        element: HTMLElement,
        options: SensorOptions,
        callbacks: SensorCallbacks
    ): SensorActivation | null;
}

interface SensorOptions {
    dragDelay?: number;
    scrollCancelThreshold?: number;
}

interface SensorCallbacks {
    onStart(initialTransform: { x: number; y: number }, element: HTMLElement): void;
    onMove(transform: { x: number; y: number }, mouseX: number, mouseY: number): void;
    onEnd(): void;
    onCancel(): void;
}

interface SensorActivation {
    initialTransform: { x: number; y: number };
    offset: { x: number; y: number };
    destroy(): void;
}
```

See the [Sensors](/examples/sensors) example for a live demo with both sensors configured.
